}}|subdivision_type1=[[Communities of Balisca|Community]]|subdivision_name1={{flag|Hesperia}}|area_km2=51,882|population_census=763,556|population_census_year=2025|regional_languages=|common_languages={{W|Aragonese language|Aragonese}}, [[Baliscano]], {{W|Catalan language|Catalan}}, {{W|Sabir language|Sabir}}|cctld=.tam|mapsize=280px|demonym=Transmontane ({{w|English language|en}}) <br> ''Transmontano'' ([[Baliscano|bl]]) <br> ''Transmuntanu/a'' ([[w:Sicilian language|scn]])|population_census_rank=39th|GDP_PPP=$28.433 billion|GDP_PPP_per_capita=$37,237|GDP_PPP_year=2025|GDP_PPP_rank=38th|GDP_PPP_rank_link=List of Baliscan regions by GDP|GDP_PPP_per_capita_rank=28th|GDP_PPP_per_capita_rank_link=List of Baliscan regions by GDP}}'''Trás-as-Montanyas''' ({{IPA|an|tɾaz uʒ ˈmõtɨʃ}}) is a geographical, administrative and cultural [[Regions of Balisca|region]] of the [[Hesperia|Hesperian]] [[Communities of Balisca|community]], within the [[Baliscan Confederation]]. The region's name means "behind the mountains", referencing it's location on the western side of the [[Matarrañya|Matarrañya / Cañadapirén]], isolated from the remainder of Hesperia. This relative isolation has led to the development of cultural affinity with [[Bayara]] and [[Estuaria]].

The economy is primarily based on agriculture, including ranching and wine production. Trás-as-Montanyas' fastest-growing sector is tourism; nearly 5 million annual tourists visit it's nature reserves. Nearly 80% of the region consists of {{W|conservation areas}}, with nearly 65% also covered by {{W|national parks}}. 

